@document.meta
title: "Devlog 2026-07-25: the parser gets its own package"
slug: norg-parser-package
date: 2026-07-25
summary: Splitting a 1,015-line parser file into five focused files behind one exported function.
tags: [devlog, neorg, refactor]
draft: false
@end

The Neorg parser lived in one file, `internal/content/norg_parser.go`, and it had grown to 1,015 lines. Metadata, block scanning, inline formatting, link checks, tables, and syntax highlighting all shared one namespace. I split it up without changing the HTML it renders.

** The boundary
The new `internal/content/norg` package exports one type and one function:

@code go
type Meta struct {
    Title   string
    Slug    string
    Date    string
    Summary string
    Tags    []string
    Draft   bool
}

func Parse(raw string) (Meta, string, string, error)
@end

`Parse` returns the metadata, the source body, and the rendered HTML. The content package converts `Meta` into posts and projects, and nothing else in the app knows a parser exists.

** The files
@table
| File | Job |
| - | - |
| `parser.go` | `Parse` and the node types |
| `metadata.go` | the `@document.meta` block and its validation |
| `blocks.go` | headings, lists, tasks, quotes, definitions, code, tables |
| `inline.go` | bold, italic, links, images, URL checks |
| `render.go` | HTML output and Chroma highlighting |
@end

** What I did not do
No interfaces, no plugin registry, no new dependencies. The tests moved next to the package first, and I watched them fail before I moved a single function.

The line count barely moved: 1,015 before, 1,019 after. I wanted five files I can read one at a time, and I got them.

The same day I moved deploys to Railway and started publishing the Docker image to GHCR.
